I'm not arguing any such thing.  
> 
> "But how do you know it is a centerfold? you ask".
> 
> Uh, because people <claim> it is when they post?  Or is that still not
> enough?

No, it's not enough.  See the subject?  What does that prove? 
Nothing.

I think Playboy is entitled to have its rights regarding
copyrighted material respected.  I don't think copyrights
should be violated.  I hope that's clear enough.  

I also think that people accused of breaking a law are entitled
to having their rights protected as well.  Remember, all we're
talking about here is some unnamed person or persons *accused* of
breaking a law.  

For all I know, Playboy has a staff of computer-literate people
examining each post, and sending messages to those persons who
post what appear to be scanned images from Playboy magazines,
telling them to cease and desist, or face legal action.  I
don't post that stuff, and as a general proposition, I don't
download it either.  

Let's also not forget that much of the attention seems to be
focussed on alt.binaries.pictures.erotica, while some people
are claiming that there are wholesale copyright violations
taking place right now on soc.men.  I have no specific
knowledge of that, either.

Today's (January 2, 1993) Boston Globe business section reports
on a number of organized attempts to defend copyright.  They do
in fact mention Playboy's action against a Jacksonville,
Florida, BBS.  According to the article, "On Dec. 9 a US
District Court judge in Florida agreed with Chicago-based
Playboy Enterprises Inc. that its copyright and trademark had
been infringed by a computerized bulletin board service based
in Jacksonville, Fla. that distributed the publisher's images
to its subscribers."  I fully expect that Playboy gave the BBS
advanced notice to cease and desist.  

More important to the future of the Internet (in my opinion) is
the suit filed by a group of writers against the New York
Times, Newsday, and Time Magazine (?among others?) alleging
copyright infringement.  According to the same Globe article,
one of the plaintiffs, Mary Kay Blakely, a writer for Ms.
Magazine, has said "What we want is a legal definition of what
the copyright law looks like in an electronic age." 

I do not advocate illegal activity by anyone.  

