Reading this discussion of what a sysadmin must be able to do and what
he/she should and should not do, etc -- esp. the comment about
encrytion reminded me of an old post (I saved it years ago when I saw
it):

) From: erik@unislc.uucp (Erik Sean Nolte)
) Newsgroups: alt.security
) Subject: Re: restricting directory so that root can not get at it
) Message-ID: <1991Dec27.205417.969@unislc.uucp>
) Date: 27 Dec 91 20:54:17 GMT
) References: <1991Dec17.181724.14054@osf.org>
) Organization: unisys
) Lines: 33
) 
) karger@osf.org (Paul A. Karger) writes:
) > |> In <138@visicom.com> makey@VisiCom.COM (Jeff Makey) writes:
) > |> 
) > |> >Just keep telling yourself over and over: there's no protection from
) > |> >the superuser ... there's no protection from the superuser ....
) > |> 
) > |>  except encryption.
) > 
) > How do you know that the superuser hasn't tampered with your encryption
) > program?
) 
) For example, you might think that you would be safe in writing your
) own encryption program and recording the checksum/size, but...
) 
) As documented in Ken Thompson, "Reflections on Trusting Trust", 
) Communications of the ACM, 27(8) [Aug. 1984], pp. 761-763,
) Thompson included a back door in /bin/login that allowed him to
) login with a predetermined password.  This back door is unusual
) in that the source code for /bin/login did not contain the trap
) door, the C compiler did!  Thompson rewrote the compiler so that
) it could detect when /bin/login was being recompiled;  as login
) was compiled, the back door code was inserted.  To make sure no
) one removed his trick from cc, Thompson enabled the compiler to
) detect when it was recompiling _itself_ and reinsert the code
) to detect the compilation of login and cc and their respective
) back door and tricks.  After compiling the new cc, the original
) cc source code was restored, thus leaving no human readable
) trace of the back door.
) 
) - Erik
) 
) [I actually read this account in Eric Raymond, _The New Hacker's Dictionary_, 
)  ISBN 0-262-68069-6, "back door", pp. 44-45]
) 

I think this shows us that there is probably no security that will let
us sleep if we really want to worry.

..regards
..walter


