Just a minute.  What Brad and I are talking about isn't prosecuting
someone, it is cleaning up a problem.  I don't need to take someone to 
court to either remove a group from my machine(s) or to try to get the 
net as a whole to pressure people to not violate copyrights on material.

A few yanked accounts would solve the problem nicely.  No due process is
necessary for that unless someone wants to make a federal case out of it, 
in which case you point out that being a prick just gets them exposed to 
the <real> justice system.  Then you let the accused take their choice; 
either stop the posting or go ahead and sue for the right to violate 
copyright and take your (much firmer) lumps in a real court.  :-)

>Let's also not forget that much of the attention seems to be
>focussed on alt.binaries.pictures.erotica, while some people
>are claiming that there are wholesale copyright violations
>taking place right now on soc.men.  I have no specific
