>to its subscribers."  I fully expect that Playboy gave the BBS
>advanced notice to cease and desist.  

But you do not KNOW that.  In fact, perhaps they didn't give them notice to
"cease and desist", and instead filed suit.  That <is> their right.  If your
material is being stolen you don't have to be nice before bringing the guns
to bear.  Responding to such a letter is sometimes worse than doing nothing,
in that if you change your pattern you <admit>, at least in some sense, that 
what you are doing is wrong (and you believe it is).  This bodes very poorly 
for you if they decide to play hardball and sue anyway after doing the 
letter-sending.

Once you <admit> to violating the copyright you have destroyed any attempt
to mount a defense.  Its then time to talk about how many dollars they want
in settlement.  If you've got the money it will be a large number.

>More important to the future of the Internet (in my opinion) is
>the suit filed by a group of writers against the New York
>Times, Newsday, and Time Magazine (?among others?) alleging
>copyright infringement.  According to the same Globe article,
>one of the plaintiffs, Mary Kay Blakely, a writer for Ms.
>Magazine, has said "What we want is a legal definition of what
>the copyright law looks like in an electronic age." 
>
>I do not advocate illegal activity by anyone.  

Good.  Then lets start cleaning up the problem, before it becomes an issue
for US sites (first) and others later on.  

