In article <4nr9jKZHGiRIyarn@netcom.com> skoper@netcom.com (Stan Koper) writes:
>Surely you're not saying that a.b.p.e. is generating 10 million
>dollars annually?  Or are you counting the $17.50 a month I
>pay to netcom (along with a few other subscribers to that and
>other commercial services)?  

Actually, I suspect USENET sites that charge money are a small part of
the E-erotica business, but yes, those sites that do charge money and
offer libraries including copyrighted stuff are most at risk, because
some portion of their revenues comes from this.  It is even more clear
if they charge hourly fees, though hourly fees are now less common on
public-access-unix sites.

>
>Is there really an E-erotica "business"?  Where is it?

There is a very large E-erotica business.  Event Horizons paid the
$500,000 penalty to Playboy and *lived*.   The fact of the BBS industry
is that the largest money makers offer downloadable nudes and porn,
often pirate, sometimes legit.   As noted, a business of providing legit
downloadable sexual images to BBSs has sprung up, largely in the wake
of cases like Playboy v. Event Horizons.

But I actually refer to the fact that a _very_ large proportion of the
business of Compuserve and the other online services (not counting Prodigy
which keeps itself G-rated) comes from downloading sexual GIF files.
And yes, a large proportion of that is pirated, and these people will be
hit by the lawsuits before any USENET site will, but do you suppose that
makes it better?

Remember this.  The porn industry is bigger than the mainstream movie
industry.   You may think it's all little shops in the combat zone and
run down cinemas, but the fact is there is more money in it than in all
the Jurassic Parks put together.

So you're actually stealing, believe it or not, the most valuable IP
there is, and the reason there aren't more lawsuits is that that industry
already has to deal with its quasi-legitimate status in society.
-- 
