I think that the heart of this argument is the old "common carrier"
status in a new form.  Whether BBS's and the internet will be treated
as common carriers is still an open legal issue, but I think that
most of us believe that they should be treated that way with regards
to e-mail.

The question now is whether systems deserve common carrier status 
with regard to news.  I think this is much trickier given that
administrators do pick and choose which newsgroups to carry.  Perhaps
only sites taking a full feed should be treated as common carriers?

Let us look at the hypothetical case of someone making color xeroxes
of Playboy and distributing 1000 copies through the mail to friends and
subscribers.  This person is breaking the law, he is violating copyrights,
he could be sued by Playboy, he could be prosecuted if caught.  He is
behaving immorally, he is stealing intellectual property.

If I see him making his copies, and I notice him mailing them, and I
have a friend who recieves them, and shows them to me, and he writes
on the envelope "Playboy Jan '94", my going to the local post master
and explaining what is going on does not create a duty on the post
master's part, either legal or moral, to stop delivery of such envelopes
with that person's return address on them.  This is becaus the US mail
is a common carrier.  With that information, I could go to the police
and cause trouble for the copier, but the post master would not be in
trouble.

If the internet is treated as a common carrier with regard to news 
the admins have
no duty to delete pirated material that is called to their attention by
users.  They have a duty to assist law enforcement personel, and the 
copyright holder or his agents, in tracking the violation to its source.

On the other hand if it is not treated as a common carrier with 
regard to news, then it seems wrong (in addition to being potentially
illegal) to willfully ignore warnings that your system is being used
for the commission of a crime.  In this case while the "it's impossible
to scan the whole feed" argument is still reasonable (whether the court
sees it that way or not), taking a look in groups like a.b.p.e (be honest
don't you really think that a lot of the traffic there is in violation
of somebody's copyright?  And did you really need Karl, or anyone else
to tell you that?) and deleting messages that a reasonable person would
expect to be copyright violations like Penthouse_01, etc, doesn't seem
terribly onerous.  If it does seem onerous, it should take all of 
fifteen minutes, if that, to determine for yourself if a large 
percentage of the traffic is of that type, and if it is, stop carrying 
the news group.

As I said to start with, this whole common carrier question is yet to
be settled in the US, and until it is (and perhaps even then) we will
just have to rely on our personal sense of the issue to guide what we 
consider to be right conduct, and to judge what is an acceptable level
of risk.


