This needs to go in the news.admin.* FAQ file.


Q: What is a common carrier?  Is the net one?

A: No.  A common carrier is a communications or transportion agency
which has applied for, and received a declaration that it is a common
carrier from (in the case of U.S. communications firms) the F.C.C.  The
common carrier is regulated by the FCC and subject to its controls on
all sorts of matters including price, levels of service and universality
of service.

In general, a common carrier is a business which carries traffic for
others, usually for a fee.  The carrier must offer the same terms of
service to all parties, and decline the business of no party (including
competing companies) if similar business is accepted from other parties.
(Thus a carrier that provided service for free would have to provide it
free to everybody, thus the fee is implicit.)

The carrier is thus absolved from most responsibility regarding what
it carries.  That responsibility lies with the originator.

This last paragraph is what excites people on the net about common carrier
status.  Unfortunately, many are not aware of the first part.

The FCC defines a different type of communications provider it calls an
enhanced services provider.  ESPs (!) are not currently regulated but
the FCC has reserved the right to do so if it feels like.

Of course, USENET and the internet are not entities, they are informal
co-ops, and as such they have no status at all, not of ESP or common
carrier.  Individual sites, particularly sites that do it as a business
such as UUNET and PSI, have ESP status.   As to whether ordinary sites
that let traffic flow for free to their friends have a status, nobody
knows, but we do known that they don't have common carrier status.
