In article <2g2oev$7b6@nic.lth.se>, Bengt Larsson <bengtl@maths.lth.se> wrote:
>
>About fair use, maybe fair use can be extended a bit for Usenet, since
>you can bring something in as part of a discussion, ie the material is
>actually a small part of the total discussion (though it may be 
>nearly all of a single message), and distributing the material is not
>why the discussion is held. 

Well, there's a difference between what fair-use allows, and the uses that
people tend to consider "fair".  This diference has existed before the net,
and will probably continue to exist.

What percentage of college/university professors' doorways or bulletin boards
do not have any copyright violations posted thereon?  How much did computer
centers pay Charles Schultz for all those Snoopy calendars they printed (and
in many cases gave away as souvenirs)?  Who has never received a photocopied
magazine/newspaper article with a yellow sticky attached, "Thought you might
find this interesting--Fred" or somesuch?

When people post to the net, they are getting a much larger audience than
when they post something on their door or mail it to a friend, but in a
sense the difference is more quantitative than qualitative.  Further, the
act of posting is still one "act".

Before one can produce a reasonable policy for what uses are "fair" on the
net, one must determine what uses of material are fair even off the net.
