I have just received second hand information from a friend who heard
on the news that Professor Matin Yaqzan, a professor of mathematics
at the University of New Brunswick in New Brunswick, Canada, has been
given a retiremen/severance package and will cease to be a professor
at UNB.  For those who have not heard about the events leading up 
to this measure: Professor Yaqzan was suspended on Nov. 11, 1993
(the same day that a presentation by Dr. Harold I. Lief, professor
emeritus of psychiatry at the University of Pennsylvania, had to 
be discontinued because of disruption by those opposed to the 
dissemination of information about 'false memory syndrome') following
his contribution of an article to the school paper, the Brunswickian.
In the article, Prof. Yaqzan outlined his view that men need a
natural outlet for sex, and that any promiscuous young woman who
accepts an invitation to a man's dormatory and gets date raped should
not express moral outrage, but merely demand monetary compensation.

Despite the eccentricity and unpopularity of his view (one to which
I do NOT subscribe), there is a problem brewing here, and a dangerous
precedent has been set (if, as I have been told, the Professor really
has been given the golden handshake): this is a situation where
a professor is being given money to stop teaching mathematics because
he has expressed his personal view on date rape --> a subject that has
nothing to do with mathematics.  No evidence has been presented, to
my knowledge, that Prof. Yaqzan's views on date rape have affected
his ability to teach mathematics, yet to date, the professor has been
suspended, and has been refused entry to university property.  The
suspension was brought on by a flood of calls received from some
students by the administration.  The calls included claims that a 
female student could feel unconfortable seeing Prof. Yaqzan in his
office given what he had written.  The ban was eventually lifted after
the union representing the university's professors threatened to file
a greivance against the suspension.  As far as I am aware, no apology
was offerred to the professor.

Essentially then, we have allowed even the university setting to be
attacked by political correctness to the extent that the freedom
to express thoughts NOT related to ones area of expertise can prevent
them from continuing to research and express the ideas they have

This represents an even more radical degradation of the protection
of freedom of thought and expression formerly guaranteed to our
professors (via tenure, and the general acceptance of free flowing
thought and expression in the university setting, independent of
unpopularity).  I say "even more" because the future of professors
who do unpopular research and express ideas related to their 
area of expertise has already been compromised (e.g., Professor
Phillip Rushton, a psychology professor at the University of
Western Ontario in Ontario, Canada, who has a controversial
theory dealing with race-differences that he says lead to differing
behavior relating to reproduction and nurturing)...Professor 
Rushton has been investigated by the R.C.M.P., has been forced
to give lectures via video tape, and has been held to an unusually
high standard in reviews of his performance as a professor and 
scholar...with poor ratings relating to little or no pay increase, 
and possibly to being 'let go'.

Social Engineering is now threatening the last, and most protected
bastion of free thought and expression (and it has for many years).
But the level of the threat is now quite high, as administrations
are displaying many signs of giving in to the forces of social
engineers, to their threats, blackmail, and labelling.  If you would
have been content to believe that the world is flat, or that the 
sun revolves around Earth, or that non-aryans are sub-human, perhaps
the drowning disruptions and political labelling techniques of the
politically fascistic don't bother you.  But if you would like to
retain the courage and governmental support to speak your mind, speak
now, or forever hold your peace.  Speak out against the use of 
black-balling labels by those who wish to silence your brothers and
sisters, speak out against those who would drown out their words with
whistles, and howls, and noisemakers.  Speak before you are no longer
allowed to do so.


