I'm not certain about US libraries, but in New Zealand all books are 
issued to members. When you apply for a library card, your age identifies
the type of membership, and the books you can borrow, and the address
of parents or guardians. If parents decide to borrow adult books on their
own card and subsequently give them to their own children, that is their 
choice. But if they gave them to their neighbours children...
Maybe US video libraries can offer all adult videos to anybody old
enough to crawl in, but in NZ there are various age classifications,
and prosecutions can arise for supplying minors.   

The point is that a child could access the catalogue, find out how
many books, but could not borrow certain books without their
parents or guardians consent. There are no such constraints on
anonymous FTP access. Maybe US libraries allow children to borrow
any book or video without parental consent. 

>Censorship is annoying.  Especially when it's badly-reasoned censorship.

The fact that so many of the alt.censorship posters see censorship when
only one person is deciding not to put it in his lending library
after being requested to consider the appropriateness of making it
available.  He doesn't prevent other libraries from  distributing it,
and the fact that _his_ library is _so popular_ is because people want
to use it. The complainers can make it available. I note that the
original uploader ( Noring - who responded to a single request in
comp.archives.msdos.d, by widely crossposting, and directing
followups here ) has made it available, but with a caution about
users in Canada ( and taking a swipe at Canada law at the same time.)

I assume other "sensistive" countries  will be chastised for their
illiberal legislation should the occassion arise. Noring could have
just made it available from his library, but probably he didn't
have the members, and so attempted to add it to more popular sites.
He seems to think that Fanny Hill should be a _cause_.
   
The _only_ connection to censorship is the artificial link created
by Noring when he directed followups here.

My opinion - FWIW ( which isn't much )
Fanny Hill is not a great literary work, _and_ I've yet to see it
in the Childrens section of a Library. It is usually classified
as an "Adult" book, and consequently Michael Regoli is entitled
to refuse it. His refusal is not censorship, it is selection,
based on his perception of what his University considers acceptable.
Interested people of all ages can obtain it from Netcom.

       Bruce Hamilton
