You totally fail to grasp the content, so don't arrogantly try
to postulate _my_ reasoning. The above is not related in _any_
way to the refusal of Fanny Hill by an _archive_ site administrator

>I enclosing the American Library Association policy on challenged
>materials and on childern's access to nonprint items.

Note, this is the Association policy, which may or may not be
adhered to as closely as governments adhere to pre-election
policies. The issue was the anonymous access site being available
worldwide, and the administrators _choice_ in not archiving the
material. New Zealand ( like some other countries ) has a government-
appointed Censor, who censors books, films, videos using criteria
that are frequently debated. It is unlikely any NZ Library would
violate that legal censorship, nor should they, as most of the
population are not sufficiently concerned about negative consequences
of censorship to request change. We could not export our policy.

And the removal of all the Enid Blyton (sp?.) Noddy books from
childrens libraries was not the result of official censorship,
but librarians responding to some public comments. The decision
was widely ridiculed in countries where it occurred. ( I've no
idea if the US even had Noddy books, but many of the Commonwealth
countries did ). Perceptions change according to the period, but
in the case of Fanny Hill, the _archive_ administrator is only
acting according to his perception of the the University policies.
He is not censoring, he has decided that Fanny Hill is not
appropriate for his archives, also knowing that it is available
elsewhere, and he is not interfering with that availability.
 
The refusal of Cica to hold Fanny Hill is their choice, and to
interpret that as censorship is quixotic.

