Michael Regoli defends his decision to remove Fanny Hill from a
publicly accessible ftp archive he maintains on Indiana University
equipment by, in essence, claiming that if he left it there
the might be negative attention to his archive and the University
would pull the plug.

I wonder if he ever considered the negative attention his action
would generate.  Years ago, on the Usenet, we used to make
up headlines describing net.events as a way of thinking through
events, but with no hope that the headlines would be written.

But, since last year, such headlines are regularly written.
The New York Times must have three Internet articles a week,
on a slow week.

So, I wonder what the powers that be at Indiana University will
think when the 60 Minutes team shoves a microphone into their
faces and asks, "Did you know that your University censors
classic literature?"  Not very far-fetched at all these days.

Hoping to hide under a rock forever is just not a viable strategy
for making one's way in this world.  If the archive here is so
tenuous that it can be killed by slight bureaucratic whim, then
by all means let it die.  But, better to go down in flames than
with a whimper.  And of course, the 3rd law of Bureaucratic Survival
states, "If you are going down, take as many people with you as you can.
That way, people will thing twice about doing it again."

-Steve Barber


 
 
