Subject:  Handling copyright violators
Date: 5 Jan 1994 20:36:31 GMT
Message-ID: <2gf8cf$e0u@olivea.ATC.Olivetti.Com>

I just did a little survey in a.b.p.e.  Of 583 articles with "begin NNN"
in them there were only 68 different posters.  What is more interesting
is that 10 of the posters are responsible for 358 (61%) of the postings.
This is pretty close to the number of reported "obvious" copyright
violations.

So I suggest that the reported "massive violations" is just one way of
looking at the data (lies, damn lies, and statistics :-) The real
problem is a small number of repeat offenders.

I suggest that social and technical mechanisms are possible for handling 
this without removing the group.  Like any other violation the best
course of action is to first contact the poster.  If they are less than
responsive one can always go the route of contacting the admin and their
feed site to try and bring pressure to bear.

But it sounds like that has been tried and in some cases the poster
and the admins involved refuse to stop.  This is not a new situation or
one limited to just copyright violations.  At one point it might have
been possible to have a site cut off but with the increased size and
comercialization of the net this is no longer probable.

What is needed is a blacklist.  I, as a news administrator, need to be
able to say that all postings from a particular user or, in the case of
a one user site, from that site should be rejected.  Not put in junk,
not passed on, but immediately discarded.

In that way someone can post to news.admin.misc that user@foosite
is posting XXX which violates YYY and told me to "F" off when I asked
him to stop.  Then, if I agree that I don't want this material on my
system, I can can add them to my blacklist.

Note that this need not be inefficient.  If it gets to the point that the
list is too big then something really is wrong.  I just don't think that
we are to that point yet.

So, Brad and Karl, what do you think of this alternative?

Censorship disclaimer:  Other peoples free speech does not extend to my
system.  I have the right to decide what articles I store on my disks
and if my downstream sites disagree with my choices they are free to
try and change my mind or find a suplimental feed.

					Jerry Aguirre
