In article <2gg63t$b96@genesis.mcs.com>,
Charles Macmillan <ilixi@MCS.COM> wrote:
>In article <2fuq8j$hrv@altitude.hip.cam.org>,
>Emru Townsend <emru@CAM.ORG> wrote:
>>
>>That the picture in question is only 1/250th of the magazine doesn't really
>>make a difference.
>
> Legally this is the case.
>
> However quoting 1/250th of a novel could be another issue.
>
> Why the different standards?
>
There really is no difference when you think about it.  Copyright applies
to works.  The novel is a single work by, generally, a single creator.  The
magazine is a work of works.  That is, each article, photo essay, or
individual photo is a distinct, copyrighted, work that is reprinted in the
magazine with permission of the copyright holder.  Furthermore, the entire
magazine itself is copyrighted as a compilation.  With magazines, many
times the copyright holder of the magazine is also the copyright holder of
some of the individual works within the magazine.  Thus, while excerpting a
portion of a novel many be fair use, reproducing a complete, individual
work from a magazine may very well not be fair use.

Just my opinions, your milelage may vary, etc, etc, etc,
Mike

