Hello, 

Several people have written to me wondering why I'm not authorizing the
distribution of my hypertext version of Fanny Hill into Canada (though I know
it's happening but I have no control over that).  Simply put, just like the
CICA archive administrator, I'm chickening out, and I admit it -- I do not
wish to become a test case, as Stephen King, the famous author, just might!
Being served with extradition papers is not my idea of fun, and my private
conversation with a couple of attornies who are following the situation say
this is a REAL possibility if I don't play it right -- thus my disclaimer that
I put into the distribution files of Fanny Hill.

The following post by Carl Kadie to soc.feminism outlines some of the reasons
why things in Canada are getting really repressive with respect to erotic
literature.  Fanny Hill is of particular focus since she is relating her
sexual experiences from the age of 15 to 18, which, according to recent
Canadian child pornography laws based on the Butler decision (see below),
makes the work illegal, despite it being fiction!  Though several have
correctly pointed out that many Canadian libraries have Fanny Hill on the
shelves at this time, some private feedback I've received is that the Canadian
government is considering the removal of Fanny Hill, as well as hundreds of
other texts, from the library shelves because they violate Canadian child
pornography laws!

Since some of the information I relate above was received privately, consider
it as not being completely reliable.  Thus, I'd like to receive more solid
information as to what's happening in Canada with regards to erotic literature
and its possible censorship.

Jon Noring

