Sean Casey <csc@pilot.njin.net> wrote:
>I am curious to know why a federally funded university feels they can
>censor things based on content. I would have thought that by receiving
>federal funds they were required to adhere to constitutional
>protection of freedom of speech. 

Does a university have an obligation to accept *every* piece of material
submitted to *every* forum?  If something is already available in another
forum, should I be obligated to accept it in mine?

Those who are crying "censorship of _Fanny Hill_ at IU!" should take a
few moments to peruse the IU online library catalog.  I found no fewer
than 6 copies at first glance, 5 of which seem to be available at this
time:

	1) Bloomington Undergraduate Library	(hardbound; reported missing)
	2) Southeast General Collections
	3) Bloomington Research Collections	(Microfiche)
	4) Bloomington Research Collections	(paperback)
	5) Bloomington Undergraduate Library	(paperback)
	6) Richmond General Collections		(paperback)
  
Time for a few assumptions -- please correct me if I am incorrect.

I assume that IU students have access to these copies; if the IU copies
are unavailable, I assume that IU students could retrieve the text via
interlibrary loan.   Furthermore, I assume that IU students have access 
to anonymous FTP; archie reveals that fanny10.zip can be retrieved from 
4 anonymous FTP archives.  

(Has _Fanny Hill_ been released by the Gutenberg Project?)

I don't believe that we can justify the assertion that _Fanny Hill_ is
being "censored," since IU students can obtain the text via several dif-
ferent methods.

While we're at it, is a university obligated to accept *any* material in a 
particular forum?  If I offer an anonymous FTP archive of, say, network maps, 
do I have to accept an upload of basketball scores?  I think not.  Should I 
be required to say in advance, "Only network maps will be accepted as up-
loads?"  I think not.   If someone uploads basketball scores and starts 
advertising that fact, am I censoring them by removing the upload?  

It should be noted that, at this time, there is only one item in the cica 
index explicitly listed as "adult."  That item is the "Hot Death UNO" card 
game; after downloading and playing it, I find the "adult" label to be a bit 
excessive for such a relatively tame card game.  I think we can say that the 
archive maintainer is being consistent; after all, we don't see any of the 
"sperm-shooting" games on that archive, right?

This "controversy" seems to be a tempest in a teapot.  No IU student
is being prevented from obtaining and reading a copy of the book; a
complaint that they can't obtain a *particular version* is a bit silly.
Would you be as upset if I complained that UK's library only offers 
_Fanny Hill_ in hardback?  If not, what's the difference?

--Wes

