While I may be outside of this argument here.... I feel you havea
slightly narrow point of view here.  If Fanny Hill is banned in the
entire Net, people would indeed care.  One, they may actually like to
d/l it, read it, u/l to other sites, etc.  This is just one of the more
"adult" electronic books out there, and in order to save face and avoid
possible scrutiny, someone decided not to allow it on their system.  And
what if the next thing banned is the e/book "Alice in Wonderland"?  It
does contain hooka-smoking catapillars (possible positive view of
drugs).
    Two, is the fact that banning any form of expression on a
particular site, or on the net as a whole is the sort of behavior which
may quickly snowball out of control. Discouraging certain forms of
behavior may be necessary at times, but censoring file directories to
avoid controversy is a somewhat stupid position to take.  If it is
illegal, then banning it is necessary.  But taking a mild erotic work
such as Fanny Hill offline only encourages those who would like to ban
all controversial works/files (i.e. Moral Majority/Concerned Parent
groups).  Then the next step is they harangue the powers that be to
remove The Anarchist's Cookbook (someone may blow something up), any
other erotic works (encourage ignorance of sex), anything not condemning
drug use (Alice included), any fantasy (might have devils/other gods).
These tactics work very well for these censors in public libraries
throughout the country, and once the censors become aware of them, will
work in public FTP/Gopher sites.
    Saying the banning of one somewhat minor work doesn't matter is just
as bad as allowing it to be banned in the first place.  And I feel we
may be in agreement with the danger of censorship on the Net, no?


