It's interesting how many people are willing to talk about whether
the removal of Fanny Hill from the CICA archive without talking
about what censorship actually is.

From a legal perspective, censorship occurs when the government
impermissibly regulates the content of press or speech.

So, first, it must be determined whether the government took
any action here.  I'm not sure I can answer this question,
but the answer isn't definitely no.  Indiana University
is for some purposes an agent of the state, and Mr. Regoli
in his capacity as an employee of the state could conceivably
take actions that would be considered state actions.

While it is true that the government need not provide its citizens
with an anonymous ftp archive, it is also true that if the government
does choose to provide one, it cannot censor, nor even "select," the
material on that archive on the basis of offensiveness.  It might be
able to restrict the contents of the archive according to a stated
purpose (like Windows programs).

Under this analysis, Mr. Regoli's statements that he "was trying to
cover [his] ass" and that it was inappropriate for a state university
to make such licentious material available probably do not pass
constitutional muster.

But my analysis is built upon a tower of suppositions, and it would
be nice if someone who knew a bit more about first amendment law
in the context of state university employees could help out here.

Unfortunately, it seems that the people actually administering
the archive know less about censorship than even my paltry
knowledge, and are acting out  of odd fears and misconceptions.



-- 
Steve Barber (sbarber@panix.com)
