According to the courts, the First Amendment applies to the states
(via the 14 Amendment) and to state institutions such as Indiana
University.

The Supreme Court cases that established this (according to _Law of the
Student Press_ by the Student Press Law Center) are:

* _West Virginia State Board of Education v. Barnette_ -- Students
      can refuse to say the Pledge to the Flag.
* _Tinker v. Des Moines_  -- Students can speak non-disruptively.
* _Healey v. James (1972) -- Public college students can form unpopular
      student organizations.
* _Papish_ (1973) -- Public college newspapers are free speech forums.
* _Widmar v. Vincent_ (1981) -- Public college student groups can be
     religious.
* _Island Tree v. Pico_ (1982) -- "[F]our of the nine justices said that
      school boards may not remove books from the school library shelves
      simply because they dislike the (sexual) ideas contained in those
      books." (4 said no. 1 didn't say.)

See my references for a list of other cases.

