Wes Morgan (morgan@engr.uky.edu) wrote:

: While universities do have an obligation to support education and
: research in a wide variety of disciplines, they also have a con-
: tractual obligation to provide their students with the courses
: necessary for graduation.  Should the contractual obligation 
: (courses required for graduation) take precedence over the more 
: esoteric offerings?   Should sociology majors be provided with
: this (seemingly elective) course while physics students have to 
: wait for openings in their *required* courses?

Hello from an Arizonan.  I've followed this case with interest.

First, if what had happened was a general ban on all electives, you might
have a point.  But that is not what happened.  The U of A offers hundreds
and hundreds of electives, many of them of a somewhat frivolous nature. 
While I don't have a current U of A schedule at hand, I do have an ASU catalog
and some old schedules, and I can attest that ASU offers dozens of
elective seminars each semester in the English department alone.  For that
matter, the P.E. department occasionally offers an elective course on how to
play billiards.  Any of these might with justification be sacrificed in
favor of a required physics class that students might otherwise be unable
to get into.  But none of these were attacked.  The course on
transsexualism was specifically singled out.  This does not indicate a
general concern for the predominance of electives over required courses;
it indicates a specific grudge against transsexualism as a subject. 
Indeed, Symington made no bones about why he wanted the course stopped;
he called it 'a slap in the face to the values of the working-class
people who support the university' [paraphrase, but 'slap in the face'
are his words].  Let's call a spade a spade.

Also, for what it's worth, I don't think there's any real tension between the
physics course and the course on transsexualism.  The scarcity of physics
courses doubtless has to do with the relative difficulty of finding physics
teachers and/or a shortage of labs.  Besides, the course in question was
taught by a graduate student who provided the instruction free of charge. 
I don't think it matters, because I don't think this was an invalid course
offering; but the cost was surely minimal.

Finally, though you didn't in fact say that the course was frivolous (you
called it 'esoteric,' which is not the same thing), I agree with Jim
Thomas that there is no reason why a course on transsexualism cannot be
valuable and useful.  I would point out that the ASU catalog includes
sociology courses on 'Courtship and Marriage,' 'Marriage Problems in
Contemporary Society,' and 'The Family.' If we find these unobjectionable
but ban courses on transsexualism, we are perilously close to limiting the
kinds of things that can be said about sexuality and relationships.  That
is a breach of academic freedom. 

Oh, yes, and as for Symington's comment that this course is a 'slap in the
face to the working class,' I'd like to know if that's the same working
class that has made superstars of Oprah and Geraldo.  

