Yes. Sorry I was unclear. It is a repost from the alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk
archive. I wrote and posted the article on 17 Apr 1993. The "case"
that inspired my research involved a moderatored mailing list. A
person was asserting that he had a legal right to have his articles
appear in the mailing list even though the moderator didn't think his
articles were relevant.

>It discusses an editor/moderator, rather than an archive
>administrator,

I see an important difference between a traditional librarian and a
traditional editor. According to the American Librarian Association
statement on "Diversity in Collection Development" [see refs]:

   Librarians have a professional responsibility to be inclusive, not
   exclusive, in collection development and in the provision of
   interlibrary loan.

Traditional editors, on the other hand, are basically exclusive rather
not inclusive. Readers don't have time to read everything; an editor
finds the best material for those busy readers.

I think see the CICA on-line archive being functionally closer a
traditional library archive (like my university's newspaper archive)
than to an edited publication. It does have a limited mission (e.g.
collecting newspapers, collecting Windows software), but within the
scope of its mission it tries to be more inclusive, not exclusive
(resources permitting).

I suppose this could be debated. But in any case ...

>but seems to indicate that the absolute right to
>exclude probably applies to Michael Regoli.

If Michael Regoli was a student (either editor or archivist) he would
likely have a absolute legal right to exclude material on even the
most arbitrary of reasons. In that case, however, exclusion of
material based on a misperception of formal university policy or (even
worse) fear of administrative or professorial retaliation would still
be very sad, legal, but sad. (And not necessarily his fault. If the
fear of retaliation is reasonable, something is wrong at the
university.)

Michael Regoli is not a student; He is a state agent. If he is like a
traditional archivist, _Island Tree_ suggests that excluding material
because some people are offended when other people read about sex may
be unconstitutional. If he is like an editor, then he likely has a
legal authority to exclude material more or less arbitrarily.  Again,
however, even if he has the legal authority to exclude material, that
doesn't make the exclusion of material based on its perceived
offensiveness morally right.

- Carl

