I just got off the phone with Ken Jensen, head of the Regina,
Saskawatchewan public library.  He told me the following
information:

* the public library in Regina is making available, through
its reference department, information about the Teale/Homolka
trial that has appeared in newspapers published in the US,
in particular the Washington Post article.

* they downloaded some information about the trial from
the Internet

* they had so many requests that they actually put together
a "reference kit" for those interested that they allow one
to peruse at the library or photocopy

* they feel they have a role or mandate to do so, based on
the fact that they subscribe to the Canadian Library
Association's statement on intellectual freedom, which reads
in part: "Libraries have a basic responsibility for the
development and maintenance of intellectual freedom.  It is
the responsibility of libraries to guarantee and facilitate access
to all expressions of knowledge and intellectual activity, including those
which some some elements of society may consider to be unconventional,
unpopular or unacceptable.  To this end, libraries shall acquire and make
available the widest variety of materials."

Here are some quotes from Mr. Jensen:

	"Removing access to material is not what a library
	 should be all about."

	"If we're going to err, it's our job to err on
	 the side of intellectual freedom."

	"We recognize we may be charged, but our job is
	 to provide information."

	"We're going to stick with being a place for
	 open access.  We're going to do what we always
	 have done."

The contrast with what happened at Waterloo is amazing, isn't
it?

If you feel like congratulating Ken Jensen for his courageous
stand, you can send him e-mail at kjensen@rpl.regina.sk.ca.

