In article <2ghf28$76m@s.ms.uky.edu>, morgan@engr.uky.edu (Wes Morgan) writes:
> Does a university have an obligation to accept *every* piece of material
> submitted to *every* forum?  If something is already available in another
> forum, should I be obligated to accept it in mine?

It is the motive for the act, not the act itself that determines if it
is censorship.  That's why it is so difficult to distinguish between
selection policy and censorship.  It depends on motive.

> I don't believe that we can justify the assertion that _Fanny Hill_ is
> being "censored," since IU students can obtain the text via several dif-
> ferent methods.

If the reason the material was removed was censorship, the availability
of the material elsewhere doesn't change the fact it was censored.  The
fact that Spycatcher was published in the U.S. doesn't change that it was
censored in the U.K.

A university can have a selection policy that says it won't store
material that is available elsewhere.  But it needs to apply that
policy consistently, and not just to certain items some people might
find objectionable.  If a university subscribes to Sports Illustrated
but makes every issue available except the swimsuit issue because
someone might be offended by the cover, it is censorship even if students
can buy the swimsuit issue at the bookstore.


> This "controversy" seems to be a tempest in a teapot.  No IU student
> is being prevented from obtaining and reading a copy of the book; a
> complaint that they can't obtain a *particular version* is a bit silly.
> Would you be as upset if I complained that UK's library only offers 
> _Fanny Hill_ in hardback?  If not, what's the difference?

If the reason why the university offered _Fanny Hill_ only in hardback is
the librarian thought the cover of the paperback too sexy, I would consider
that censorship.  Even if the university bought it in hardback.  On the
other hand, if the reason the university didn't buy the paperback is its
policy to only buy the hardback when available because it lasts longer,
I would not consider it censorship as long as it was consistently applied.

Remember, its the motive for the action, not the action itself that
determines if it was censorship.  Mr. Regoli's original post seemed to
indicate that Indiana University policy was being inconsistently applied
in the removal of _Fanny Hill_.  Several other posters from Indiana
University have given other reasons why it was removed.  The original
post appeared to indicate a case of censorship.  The later posts appear
to indicate a poorly applied selection policy.

I doubt we'll ever know exactly what the motives of all the participants
were.
