I've been following this bruhaha for several days now, and it looks like
everyone has completely lost track of almsost all the relevant issues in
all the rhetoric.

Heres the important aspects, as I see it.

1. Was the removal censorship?
Well, yes - it was. According to my dictionary, anyway. My dictionary
tells me that a "censor" is "an official who examines books or films for
objectionable matter." This is what the gentleman in question did, and in
so doing he engaged in censorship. Censorship is not, however, allways
illegal, immoral or intellectually dishonest (somebody raised the example
of a mosque that refuses to keep books offensive to Islam in its library
- that's censorship too, but it's completely resonable censorship). It seems
pretty clear by now that the removal of Fanny Hill was not STATE censorship
in any way, so it probably wasn't illegal. Let's skip "immoral" for the
moment and move right on to "intellectually dishonest."

2. Was the removal arbitrary or dishonest? Well, this depends on the
stated policy of the archive - something which seems to change every time
I've seen it posted so far. If the stated policy was "no obscene or
pornographic material" on the archive, then there were no grounds for
eliminating Fanny Hill - a book which has been declared not obscene over
and over again at the very highest levels of government and academia. If
the stated policy was "no adult material" (i.e. material complete
unsuitable for minors) then the removal was entirely defensible. Fanny
Hill is certainly an "adult" book.

3. Finally, was the removal immoral? Well, this depends on your personal
morality. If you define morality as "taking a firm stand against all
sexual indecency" then maybe it was moral. If you define morality as
"remaining always open to new ideas, no matter how dangerous or offensive"
then the removal was extremely immoral. If the book was removed just
because some moralistic demagogue with an axe to grind might use it to
make trouble for the archive with the university bigwigs, however, the
removal was neither moral or immoral, but just gutless. Defensible,
possibly, maybe even necessary, but gutless nonetheless. 

So, if the removal was illegal, dishonest or immoral, what can we do? 

Well, if it was illegal we could bring charges and seek statuatory
redress, but since it seems pretty well established that it wasn't, the
point is moot.

If the removal was dishonest - i.e. not in accord with the archive's state
policies, I suppose it's technically possible that we might complain to
the university and seek administrative solutions, but does any body really
want to cause the service that kind of grief? Realistically, the only
constructive thing to do is to encourage the administrators to decide what
their policies really are, spell them out in writing, then stick by them.

If the removal was immoral, then we can explain why we found it immoral
and try to reasonably persuade the administrators to come around to our
