morgan@engr.uky.edu (Wes Morgan) writes:

>Is there a difference between one who is specifically employed to act
>in such a capacity and one who is simply "doing it on his time" with
>tacit approval?  In other words, can a person employed as a state
>agent wear other hats as well, or does their status as state employee
>affect *everything* they do?

I think staff can and should be able to do things on their own time.
It is my impression that every university has a staff handbook, this
would be the place to start looking. I know that here at U. of
Illinois staff can form and join campus organizations. Such
organizations are allowed to use some university resources and very
definitely don't represent the university.

[...]
>Let's suppose that, with tacit approval, I start an Internet service
>(FTP archive, mailing list, whatever) which I manage on my own time.
[...]

I think this can be either done either messily or neatly. As an
example of "messy", consider the situation here at the U. of Illinois
CS department last October. The mailing list reflector for
rec.arts.erotica was shut down. It has been set up by a department sys
admin. It was shut down by the department on the grounds that it was
"not a necessary allocation of the resources involved." Who judges
this? I don't know. Does that mean that the department now certifies
all resource allocation is "necessary"? I don't know. The sys admin's
ended his announcement with the request "Please do not ask for any
more information regarding this matter."

It would have been much better to have a policy on mailing lists and
archives. I would think a great model for such a policy would be the
student/staff organizations policy. Such a policy would not give
computer staff any privileges beyond those given to other staff and
students, rather it would give everyone a equal opportunity chance to
set up a mailing list or archive. Also, the university would certainly
regulate such a use of its resources (time, place, manner), but such
regulation would be content neutral.

- Carl

